  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Friday)\nIssued at 1147 AM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nIt has been another clear sky day with hazy conditions. Latest air\nquality index is at the Moderate level with some isolated spots in\nthe Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups. Looking ahead, a passing cool\nfront will give some low-end shower and storm chances for parts of\nthe southern SD plains this evening. Locally heavy rainfall rates,\nlightning, and strong gusty winds are the main concerns.\nAfterwards, skies will be quiet as temps cool to the upper 50s to\nlower 60s.\n\nSunday will be an interesting day to see play out. Machine\nlearning severe weather probability models have been highlighting\nan elevated risk for severe weather on Sunday for multiple days\nnow. But hi-res short term models are hesitant to allow convection\nto happen. Mostly likely for several reasons, like how dry it is\nand how warm the atmosphere will be. But either way, the consensus\nis, if storms are able to get going, they could be strong to \nsevere. The SPC has placed a Marginal over most of the SD \ncounties with a Slight over the central SD counties. If storms are\nable to initiate, thinking is that they would begin near and in \nthe Black Hills before drifting eastward into the SD plains. Main \nconcerns would be wind and lightning.\n\nOtherwise, skies will be on the sunnier side with lingering haze\ntomorrow. Afternoon temps will reach the low to mid 90s for most,\na little cooler for some in the Black Hills and in northern SD.\n\nMonday and Tuesday look similar, with highs mostly in the upper \n80s to lower 90s. Zonal flow will keep the daily chances for\nafternoon storms in place over the CWA. \n\nUnsettled pattern under zonal flow will give the area cooler \ntemps and daily chances for rain and storms for the second half of\nthe week.",
